00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.710
- Hello, everyone.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.993
Welcome to the Academy's
Visiting Artist Film Series,

00:02:58.993 --> 00:03:01.230
FilmAid edition for makeup and hair.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.680
My name's Howard Berger.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.470
I'm a makeup artist
specializing in makeup effects.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.470
And today I have the privilege of speaking

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.700
with the amazing and
talented makeup artist

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.517
and overall fantastic
person, Lois Burwell,

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:20.510
who has worked in this
industry for a while

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.280
and has done amazing things.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.920
Lois won an Oscar Academy Award

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.100
for her work in "Braveheart."

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.907
She was nominated for her
work in "Sing Private Ryan."

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.690
She served as a branch governor
and she is truly a leader

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.680
in the film industry,
leader at the Academy.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.260
We all love her dearly and
you know, it takes more

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.490
than just being great at
your job to be successful.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.510
You have to be a great person.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.620
And this is something that Lois is,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.260
all these things, she really is.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.883
So it's wonderful to see you, Lois,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.350
thank you for joining us.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.410
This is gonna be fun.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.880
We're gonna learn all about
your life and thoughts

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.540
and all the magic that you
bring to the film industry

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.280
and, and to all of us in our industry.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.130
So how are you today?

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.380
- Well, after that
introduction, I'm blown away.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.170
I'm kind of disappointed by being me now,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:15.862
'cause the introduction
(Howard laughs)

00:04:15.862 --> 00:04:16.907
is so fantastic.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.050
I'm all right today.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.103
It's a rainy day here
and that's rather nice.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:23.930
- It is. Yeah.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.090
Very nice. Very nice.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.747
Well, thank you for joining us.

00:04:26.747 --> 00:04:29.687
You and I have been friends
for a very long time.

00:04:29.687 --> 00:04:33.350
We first met on "Green
Mile," on "The Green Mile"

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.950
and you taught me a lot about leadership

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:40.570
and being inclusive and
working together as one

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.422
because sometimes being
a makeup effects person

00:04:43.422 --> 00:04:47.130
and being a makeup
artist has the perception

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.900
of two different worlds, but it doesn't,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.980
we work together and all these years,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.990
you and I have worked
together as colleagues

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.150
and as very, very good friends.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.280
So what I wanna do first is let's start,

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.780
let's talk about the beginning,

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.160
'cause this is always very interesting,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.960
'cause you have a very
interesting start in your career.

00:05:04.272 --> 00:05:06.360
You were born in North London

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.020
and which is thousands of miles away

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.750
from Hollywood, California, and I'm sure

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.190
at some point you were like,
"This is gonna be impossible,"

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:17.503
but you always attacked the
impossible with possibilities.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.790
Let's talk a little bit
about how it all started

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.770
because also you didn't
begin the standard way

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.010
UK makeup and hair artists
and stylists begin,

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:30.320
which sometimes is through the
intense structure of the BBC.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.690
You went a different route and let's,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.160
yeah, share with us a little
bit about what that was like,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.200
how it all came, came about, Lois?

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.660
- Well, the thing is I was born into

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.630
a working class family
where my mother worked

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.930
actually for the police as a telephonist.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:54.889
And before that as a, Grosvenor
House, as a telephonist

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:58.420
and my father was an electrician,
a household electrician.

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:02.090
So there was never a thought of a career

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.970
that would be possible within makeup.

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.830
I mean, it wasn't
something that, now I think

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.790
it's much more know and talked about

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.630
especially with social
media and everything.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.257
So there's an advantage there,
but when I was growing up,

00:06:15.257 --> 00:06:19.523
it was something that was beyond
my dream or comprehension.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.810
I started really when
I was at dance school

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.840
at the evenings and at weekends,
just a local dance school

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.193
called the Iris Caldecott School of Dance.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.247
And when I was about five or six,

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.230
our class was doing a
"Dance of the Dolls,"

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.950
and everyone was turning up to rehearsal,

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.403
not looking like a doll.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:49.970
So I started to interfere
and basically paint

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.230
the other fellow students as dolls.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.620
And it just started from there.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.940
And I thought, I really enjoyed this.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:58.850
I liked doing it.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:02.240
I still didn't think that
there'd ever be a career in it,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.980
but I would just tinker
around at home and see things

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.140
that were in films that
would inspire me and think,

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.290
how would you do that?

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:09.880
How can you do that?

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.430
And just work it out with
a very simple stage makeup

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.450
'cause that's what I had.

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.367
And then when I got to a point where

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:20.077
I was about to leave school and I thought,

00:07:20.077 --> 00:07:21.760
"Well, should I apply to the BBC?"

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:23.720
but I didn't want to work in television.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.310
And I know that sounds really,

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.950
I think back and I think
what a fool I was anyways,

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.130
but I just, I didn't want to do it.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.470
I wanted to work in film.
Film was where my heart was.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:37.100
Film is so exceptional because,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.540
and I'm sure that's why
everyone's drawn to it

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.780
because you sit in that dream-like state,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.200
you sit in the dark with strangers

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.150
and everything in your
life stops in that moment.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.209
And you are taken to a different
world, to different lives,

00:07:52.209 --> 00:07:56.383
having a different experience
from your own life.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.680
So that's what I wanted to do.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.310
I didn't want to be confined within a box

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:06.480
in the living room, which is
how I perceived television

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.869
as being at that time.

00:08:08.869 --> 00:08:13.869
So I started to write
to fellow film students

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:18.730
and offer my services,
working on films for free.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:22.243
And that's basically how I started.

00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:24.054
- Hmm.

00:08:24.054 --> 00:08:25.090
- Now how did you-
(crosstalk)

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:26.260
Oh, go ahead, I'm sorry. No no-

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:30.000
- And so what happened was
the National Film School-

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.910
- Hm mm?
- when you left,

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.989
you had a union card, so
you graduated and were given

00:08:35.989 --> 00:08:39.470
more or less access to
work, not the job itself,

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:42.590
but access to work because the
union was really strict then

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:47.297
in the UK and you could not
work without a union card.

00:08:47.297 --> 00:08:50.300
and you had to have a union
card in order to work.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.440
So it was a catch 22, unless
you had an "in" somewhere,

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.150
where of course I obviously didn't.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.390
Anyway, what happened was
the students would be pleased

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:00.190
with the work I did, but it
wasn't just doing makeup.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:01.450
I mean, it was everything.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:04.170
It was everything from editing,

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.690
working on a Steenbeck
to taking over sound

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.337
when the sound chap went out,

00:09:10.337 --> 00:09:11.170
(Howard chuckles)

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:12.901
for a packet of cigarettes
and we never saw him again.

00:09:12.901 --> 00:09:15.140
It was all those kind of things.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.470
So it was a real, "I've got the frocks,

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.300
you've got the music,
let's put on a show,"

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:20.250
it was one of those.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.150
And when they left, some
of them got into doing

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:29.830
what was then rock videos,
you know, pop videos,

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:31.830
'cause it was the beginning of that then.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.653
That's how old I am just
so everyone can work

00:09:33.653 --> 00:09:35.663
that out on their fingers now, anyway.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.980
They asked me to work on them and then

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.250
of course you experimented
and it was a time,

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.160
especially with rock
videos of doing elaborate

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.313
or extraordinary or different kind of,

00:09:51.313 --> 00:09:53.450
you know, that punk rock
through to glam rock,

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.073
all those different looks.

00:09:56.369 --> 00:09:58.060
So that's how I started.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.040
And then literally I was on a student film

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.350
coming back from Scotland and we stopped

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:06.210
at a pub in on the way down.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.200
Obviously I was of the legal age

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.581
to have an alcoholic
beverage at that time.

00:10:10.581 --> 00:10:11.414
(both chuckling)
Anyway.

00:10:11.414 --> 00:10:15.640
So we stopped at the
pub and it was a very,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.037
it was the filmmakers pub in Glasgow.

00:10:18.037 --> 00:10:21.060
So everyone knew everyone else.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.177
And two chaps came in
and one I was talking to,

00:10:25.177 --> 00:10:29.901
we just struck up a conversation
about filmmaking in general

00:10:29.901 --> 00:10:32.228
and I never spoke to his friend.

00:10:32.228 --> 00:10:35.550
And then a year later I get a phone call

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:38.240
from a lovely production
manager called Patty Hickson,

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.370
who's no longer with us, but
was a lovely woman, anyway.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:44.391
So she telephoned me and she said,

00:10:44.391 --> 00:10:47.570
"Lois, are you interested in
doing Bill Forsyth's film,

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.480
'Gregory's Girl?'"

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.795
And I said, "I don't know Bill Forsyth!"

00:10:51.795 --> 00:10:56.240
And she said, "Well, he knows
you, because you were talking

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.860
to his friend, Mike, who
happened to be Mike Radford

00:11:00.147 --> 00:11:04.000
in the Holt on your way
back from Brian's film."

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.290
And I said, "Really?"

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.085
I mean, I had no had no recollection,

00:11:10.085 --> 00:11:11.960
(Howard laughs)
but he'd obviously

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.900
seen the work and thought
that it would be good

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:16.150
to work with me.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.150
So that's how I started.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:21.000
And that makes it sound
like you step out the door

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.240
and that's it, you're an
instant makeup artist.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.001
The one thing I really
want to inspire everyone

00:11:26.001 --> 00:11:29.900
is that a certain amount
of it is determination,

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:34.900
but it's also self determination
in you constantly work

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.420
to push the envelope.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.600
I mean, I still want to
be a good makeup artist.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.630
I still look forward to
being a good makeup artist.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.760
I don't believe a hundred
percent that I am right now,

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.805
because I can always improve.

00:11:49.805 --> 00:11:53.550
You can't rest on your
laurels is what I'm saying.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:58.550
So when I started it
was just the beginning

00:12:01.001 --> 00:12:04.390
and I had no idea of
where anything would go

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:06.700
or where it would lead to it.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.401
Wasn't a thought of, "Right,
now I'm going to Hollywood."

00:12:09.401 --> 00:12:14.401
that has actually has never
been a part of my agenda.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:20.100
It's always been this job
or that interesting film,

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.040
this collaboration, this feels right.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.333
And I think that to a certain degree,

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.350
if there's something I've learned,

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.340
it's that if something feels wrong,

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.280
then it's better if you can not to do it.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.770
- Hm mm, I agree.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:40.500
Yeah. I always feel it's I
never regret saying "No,"

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.940
but I regret saying "Yes."

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:43.930
- So...
- Yeah.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.803
- Yeah. Were your parents supportive?

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:51.167
- They were supportive in that
they were supportive in that,

00:12:51.167 --> 00:12:53.360
"Yes, have a go at that,"
but they didn't believe

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.660
it could be a career.
- Right!

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.950
- Because it was beyond
their comprehension.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:01.340
In fact, there's a rather amusing story.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:03.550
I did a film very early on in my career

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:06.710
with Glenda Jackson called "Gyro City."

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:09.260
And Glenda Jackson's a wonderful actress.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.800
And my mom of course,
absolutely adored her

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:15.780
in "Elizabeth R," which
was a television program

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.260
that she was exceptional in and everyone

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.760
throughout the country was just

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.683
enamored with her and quite rightly so,

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:26.927
but I was working on the
film and mom said to me,

00:13:26.927 --> 00:13:30.610
"You know, when you are
there doing the makeup,

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:32.973
do you actually touch her?"

00:13:32.973 --> 00:13:35.590
(Howard chuckles)

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:36.590
Couldn't believe it.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.050
- It's funny though. Yeah.

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:43.240
But it is important, like you were saying,

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.300
you need to believe in
yourself, but it certainly helps

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.100
when you have a family and parents too,

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.900
that help support you and
let you go down this journey.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.363
So there's a lot to it.

00:13:56.359 --> 00:13:57.800
- Mind you Howard, I
don't know about that?

00:13:57.800 --> 00:13:59.577
Because there was always that thing about

00:13:59.577 --> 00:14:03.068
"You must do something,
so you have a proper job."

00:14:03.068 --> 00:14:04.440
- Of course. Yeah.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.650
Even I think now, even
after all these decades,

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:08.237
people still like, "So
what, do you still do that?"

00:14:08.237 --> 00:14:09.900
"Do you still make monsters?"

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:13.110
I'm like, "Yeah, I do."
It's been 40 years.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:14.740
I think I'm probably gonna stick with it.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:15.670
- Yeah, exactly.

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:16.670
- Absolutely.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.860
You know, in our industry we
do a lot of collaborating.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.023
We have to, we can't just be an island.

00:14:23.087 --> 00:14:25.750
And, just gonna talk a little
bit about "Braveheart,"

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:28.870
since that's the film that you
won your Academy Award for,

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:32.130
but on that film, you met
your husband, John Toll,

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:35.350
who was the cinematographer,
amazing cinematographer.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:39.450
Great, great, brilliant
artist who Mel Gibson says

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.520
is the greatest cinematographer alive.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.870
So, which is true, but it's interesting,

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.680
because you've done a
couple movies with John.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.080
So what's that collaboration
and working relationship with

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.930
'cause he's obviously your
husband, you're his wife,

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.590
but you have to feel
confident to put something

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.500
in front of the camera
that you feel good that,

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:59.800
you know he's looking at it
through his view view finder,

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:03.580
but also you have to trust
him to present it properly,

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.700
in the way you see it too.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:07.430
As far as collaboration
goes, let's start there.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:08.480
Let's talk about the collaboration

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:11.000
between the cinematographer
and the makeup artist.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.433
- Well, that's how we met.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:16.243
I look back at it and it was very clear.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:22.350
It wasn't like going on a date
or, or a romantic encounter.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:25.800
That isn't what it was about.
- Right?

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.610
What it was about was
when you start on a film,

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:31.770
obviously I was quite nervous
and you want to do a good job

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:35.907
and you want to get the
character exactly right

00:15:35.907 --> 00:15:37.620
and all those things.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.760
And when I first met
John, actually, I thought

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.350
he was the operator because
he was operating the camera

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:44.060
and it was a makeup test.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.830
And he looked up from behind the camera

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.850
and he kind of scowled
at me, kind of frowned.

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:53.210
It was my thought, I don't
like the look of him,

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:54.970
but actually what it was,
was that he didn't have

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.050
his glasses on so he was squinting

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.576
to try and get me in focus.

00:15:58.576 --> 00:16:01.230
(both chuckling)

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.690
But literally, how we came together

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:06.720
was that, I mean there wasn't
time to chat on a film set.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:08.750
It wasn't like we were
talking to each other

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:11.430
about anything other than the work.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.100
So it was always work based and we'd go

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.780
to rushes every night, so
we'd see dailies every night.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.420
And in the beginning, obviously everyone

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:22.635
was really enthusiastic and
there'd be like 35 people,

00:16:22.635 --> 00:16:26.220
going, or 40 people going to
dailies to see their work.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:28.950
And then by the end of Week
2, when the hours are long

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:31.270
and the weather was really inclement,

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.112
that by the end of the second week,

00:16:34.112 --> 00:16:34.945
there were like four of us.

00:16:34.945 --> 00:16:38.210
So that, and it was
always myself and John.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:42.700
So we would discuss, I remember one thing,

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:45.770
was that obviously, Mel was
meant to be in the script.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:48.920
It had him described
Wallace, William Wallace,

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:52.000
as being described as
in his early twenties.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:56.040
Well, Mel was in his early
forties and he was directing,

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.660
producing as well as starring in the film.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.950
So he wasn't getting a lot of sleep.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:04.850
So instead of looking
all refreshed and heroic,

00:17:04.850 --> 00:17:07.292
he was looking rather wrinkly and tired,

00:17:07.292 --> 00:17:09.317
(Howard laughs)
to be perfectly honest.

00:17:09.317 --> 00:17:11.570
And John said to me, can you do something

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:15.440
to make him look more youthful?

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:18.707
And I said, "Well, I'm doing the best."

00:17:18.707 --> 00:17:23.270
But poor soul, you know, there's
only X amount you can do.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:27.810
And in fact, it was John's
birthday when we first

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:31.140
started shooting and I had found

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:35.380
an empty packet of cigarettes and I put

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:39.747
gaffer tape around them and
wrote on them as little present,

00:17:39.747 --> 00:17:43.387
"Instant wrinkles, guaranteed to annoy."

00:17:43.387 --> 00:17:46.853
"Hollywood tested by John
Toll," and gave them to him!

00:17:47.693 --> 00:17:48.526
(both chuckling)

00:17:48.526 --> 00:17:53.060
and I suppose that's
how we began to actually

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:54.940
see each other as people.
- Yeah.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:59.260
- Rather than as film colleagues,

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:00.940
but it was always about the work.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.100
So when I've worked with
him on other things, I mean,

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.320
I know to trust him because
we we've trodden that path.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:09.470
And in actual fact we've worked on films,

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:12.250
when we did "Captain Corelli's Mandolin,"

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.180
the two lead actors, not Penelope Cruise,

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:18.890
but two others, had no idea
that we were a married couple,

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:23.470
none, until about four or
five weeks into the shoot,

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.273
because there was nothing on the,

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:29.860
when we are at work that
would tell anyone that.

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:31.550
- Right. Which is the way to do it.

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:33.498
Yeah. You don't want to get it, so-

00:18:33.498 --> 00:18:34.331
- Yeah.

00:18:34.331 --> 00:18:37.010
- Do you feel that being
married to John and working

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:39.944
with him, it's helped you
with other cinematographers,

00:18:39.944 --> 00:18:44.944
in terms of what a
cinematographer expects,

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:50.420
I know you hold yourself
at very high level,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.197
you're very, very specific about things

00:18:52.197 --> 00:18:54.160
and you always wanna
make sure it's brilliant.

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.780
So, but yeah, it must
be, you're used to John

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:00.460
and then go to set for
another DP and you know?

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:04.080
- Yeah, but that can that
can be a double edged sword

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.560
to be perfectly honest
because the people that know,

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.680
I mean, most people don't
know when I go to work,

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:09.600
who I'm married to.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:11.900
I mean, I was working
with Steven Spielberg.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.470
We weren't married John and I at the time,

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:16.380
but we were dating and it was almost

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:20.080
the penultimate day of
"Saving Private Ryan,"

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:23.780
before Steven knew that
I was with or Janusz knew

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:25.280
that I was with John Toll!

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:31.497
So, there's that, but
when you, sometimes it's,

00:19:31.497 --> 00:19:34.286
"Oh, she thinks she knows
because she's married "to."

00:19:34.286 --> 00:19:35.119
- Right.
- I mean, I remember

00:19:35.119 --> 00:19:38.970
an AD who was married to a makeup artist

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:40.730
who used to come in and give notes

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.800
on how to make up people in the background

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:44.633
or small characters.
- Hm mm?

00:19:44.633 --> 00:19:47.873
- And I said, "Excuse me,
where is this coming from?"

00:19:47.873 --> 00:19:49.737
"Is this from the director?"

00:19:49.737 --> 00:19:52.700
"Are you passing on a
note from the director?"

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.420
And he said, "No, I just
think it is a good idea."

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.897
And I said, "Look, you're
married to a makeup artist."

00:19:57.897 --> 00:19:59.700
"You're not a makeup artist and you're

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:02.469
not the head of department, go away."

00:20:02.469 --> 00:20:04.230
(both chuckling)

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:06.060
So I understand,-
- Right?

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.360
- and I don't want ever to be that person,

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.300
but obviously you have
to work collaboratively.

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:14.700
I mean, whenever I start work on a film,

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:19.110
the port of call is the
script, to read the script

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:23.240
and to read it as if you
are sitting in the cinema,

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:26.890
watching it, that's the way I do it.

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:29.110
And then I do a breakdown,

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:31.120
a technical breakdown of the script,

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:32.520
which I call the makeup script.

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:35.798
So it's, you know how I
mean, the day passes,-

00:20:35.798 --> 00:20:37.330
- Right?
- Are they tired?

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:38.340
Have they got red eyes?

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:40.326
Is it hot out? Is it cold out?

00:20:40.326 --> 00:20:41.860
Have they had a wash?

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:43.810
You know, (chuckles) all those questions

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.560
you ask yourself about
every single character.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:50.520
And so having done that,
then met with the director

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:55.520
and seeing where our ideas
merge or if we can work together

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:59.233
because there's no point
trying to be the ugly sister,

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:03.380
cramming your foot in Cinderella's
shoe, there is no point.

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:04.980
You're never gonna get anywhere.

00:21:06.486 --> 00:21:08.510
Or it won't be fulfilling.
- Hm mm, hm mm.

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:10.460
And then you let the film down.

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:12.070
There's nothing to say you are always

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:14.826
the right person for the job.

00:21:14.826 --> 00:21:18.057
I think that's important to recognize.

00:21:18.057 --> 00:21:21.860
So after that having met the director,

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:24.650
then the next port of
call is production design,

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:26.870
because I want to see the overall vision

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:28.240
that what they're working on.

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:31.960
And then it's the
cinematographer, before costume,

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:36.107
before meeting the actor, it
has to be the cinematographer.

00:21:36.107 --> 00:21:38.750
- Got it. Yeah, no, I agree.

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:41.403
There's definitely the
succession of it all.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:45.543
Yeah. It is, it's a giant
collaborative effort,

00:21:46.831 --> 00:21:48.530
and even here in the United States,

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.580
there's a delineation
between hair and makeup,

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:54.360
but overseas, there's
not, for the most part.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:55.973
It's one and the same.

00:21:57.140 --> 00:22:01.450
And yeah, you need to work
with a great hair stylist

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:04.027
designer who you feel good about as well,-

00:22:04.027 --> 00:22:04.860
- Yeah!

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.340
- because it all comes together.

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:09.430
There's so much that has
to be taken into account,

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.010
and then you have the actor.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:11.843
- Yeah.

00:22:11.843 --> 00:22:12.676
- You know?
- Exactly.

00:22:12.676 --> 00:22:14.076
- It's like you feel-

00:22:14.076 --> 00:22:15.260
- And then that's where you all meet.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.457
It's big jigsaw puzzle, that's
how I always think of it.

00:22:18.457 --> 00:22:19.790
- Yeah. It is.

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:23.180
- And you're just a little
piece of that jigsaw puzzle,

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:25.900
but sometimes you can be
the most important piece,

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.453
you know?
- Oh, absolutely.

00:22:28.453 --> 00:22:30.530
- I think that's true of everyone,

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.551
and the other extraordinary thing,

00:22:33.551 --> 00:22:35.670
I dunno about you Hal, but
I've found over the years

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:40.120
that there's a kind of a confusion between

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:43.433
being a colleague and being a friend,

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.580
and they're not
necessarily the same thing.

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:49.100
People bandy around the
term, "Oh, my friend, this,

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:51.400
my friend," no, your colleague.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:55.590
And sometimes you can be
collegial and work exceptionally

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:58.960
well with someone, but not
particularly warm to them

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.560
in any other context
other than being at work.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:07.770
And I think starting out
that can be very confusing

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:11.460
when you are brand new.

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:12.293
- Yeah.

00:23:12.293 --> 00:23:15.240
- And I think it can be
very disappointing too,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:20.240
because in any artistic
realm, you have to be prepared

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:26.710
to take risks, and to take
risks you have to be prepared

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:31.710
to fail and to take the
wrong choice of a makeup

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.453
or take the wrong choice of a job even.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:43.410
But it's all part of a
learning curve including, that,

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:45.210
and I'm sure it's been said to you,

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:47.080
and I know it's been said to me,

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:51.150
"I'm going to have you work
with me on all of my films."

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:52.570
- Oh yeah.

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:54.670
- And then you never
see that person again.

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:55.867
- Hm mm.
- You know?

00:23:55.867 --> 00:23:56.700
- No.

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.160
- And then the surprise
can be that the person

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:02.370
that you've never, that
you were just being

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:07.370
regular collegial,
decent to, is the runner

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:09.710
who becomes the studio head.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:11.480
- Hm mm.
- Or...

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.430
- Very true.
- The producer, you know?

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:19.440
I think that working
collaboratively with anyone,

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:21.390
no matter how talented they are or not,

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:27.930
I think you have to recognize
the humanity in everyone

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.370
and embrace it and make it a part

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:35.490
of your ethic of work.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:39.233
Only because if you...

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:43.200
I'm trying to think how
to say this to students,

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.130
hopefully some of whom are going to be

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:48.233
incredibly successful, makeup
artists and hair stylists.

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:54.540
I think you can't spend 40 years.

00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:56.840
I mean, I've been doing
it for more than 40 years

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:00.250
as you have had, and
you can't spend 40 years

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:01.653
being someone else.

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:06.060
You can only, because it's disrespectful,

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:07.870
I think to your own life.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:12.163
And it's confusing to your growth.

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:17.730
It's very important that
you are truthful to yourself

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:21.493
as well as to others when asked.

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:25.490
And I think, and I could be wrong,

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:30.140
but I think that's the best
recipe for a successful life

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:33.430
and a successful career,
to be perfectly frank.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:35.420
- No, I agree. I think
that's great advice.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:37.850
'Cause sometimes I think we get caught up

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:40.860
in the inertia of it all
and you focus just on this

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:42.460
and it's bigger than that.

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:45.250
And your department, the film is bigger.

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:47.020
Life is bigger than just your department.

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:48.530
And sometimes we just focus on like,

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:49.363
it's gotta be this,

00:25:49.363 --> 00:25:50.196
it's gotta be this.
- Yeah!

00:25:50.196 --> 00:25:52.773
You know, it's also, I think
what you were saying is,

00:25:52.773 --> 00:25:56.890
I find it's much easier to be
nice to everybody than not.

00:25:56.890 --> 00:25:58.550
And you're right, you don't know.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:03.550
I worked with a PA in the 80s
and he's a big director now,

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:04.890
and I was always nice to him.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:07.102
He hasn't hired me, but that's okay.

00:26:07.102 --> 00:26:08.640
(Lois chuckles)
- But nonetheless, you know,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:09.473
but you know what?

00:26:09.473 --> 00:26:12.920
I watch people in our
industry who sometimes

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:17.370
aren't very nice and
you're gonna be remembered

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:19.343
for how you made people feel,

00:26:20.471 --> 00:26:24.360
and what they take away
from the experience.

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:28.000
And it's very important to
treat everybody with respect.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.070
It's a very good bit of advice, Lois,

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:31.917
it's actually the best, you
know, it's better than like,

00:26:31.917 --> 00:26:34.860
"Oh, learn how to do brows
or learn how to do this."

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:37.481
It's like, just be a really
good person, you know?

00:26:37.481 --> 00:26:38.314
- Yeah.

00:26:38.314 --> 00:26:39.770
- It makes a world of difference.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:43.686
And obviously, you've worked with people

00:26:43.686 --> 00:26:47.440
time and time again, who
value you on all levels.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:51.850
And as a person, as an
artist and as a contributor

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:53.790
and a team member, cause
that's really what you are.

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:56.780
You're not someone who just
comes and you do the job.

00:26:56.780 --> 00:26:58.400
And then you sit in a chair all day long

00:26:58.400 --> 00:26:59.460
and look at a magazine.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.730
You are a team member and you're
part of the creative team.

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:05.130
And that's something that
we really try to push.

00:27:05.130 --> 00:27:07.160
You know, you're not a vendor.

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:11.840
You obviously have worked quite
a bit with Steven Spielberg

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:15.490
and on movies like "Lincoln"
and "Ready Player One"

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:16.790
and "Warhorse," and of course

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:18.490
blockbuster "Saving Private Ryan"

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:23.490
and he obviously values you tremendously.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.540
And would you just
share a little bit about

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.300
what it's like working with him, you know,

00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:31.440
'cause he's one of my all
time favorite film directors

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:35.170
in the world and I just
think it's wonderful

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:36.910
that you get to work
with him all the time.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:38.850
So talk a little bit
about that if you would.

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:42.360
- Well, I think it's one of
the biggest joys of my career,

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:43.193
to be honest.

00:27:43.193 --> 00:27:46.780
I mean, I have other personal
joys or little things

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:49.710
that I like about
certain things, but that,

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.067
working with Steven is fabulous.

00:27:52.067 --> 00:27:57.067
And in fact, I was a huge
Steven fan, I mean massive

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:02.340
and went in, trampling,
the Queen's crisp packets

00:28:02.787 --> 00:28:07.210
and popcorn boxes after
the Royal performance,

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:10.163
to be the first in there
to see "Close Encounters"

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:12.583
when I was a teenager.

00:28:16.166 --> 00:28:18.110
I didn't ever think that
I'd be working with him.

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:22.400
And I tell you the first
job that we worked together,

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:23.680
well, I say we worked together.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:27.840
We didn't work together at
all, I was doing the crowd

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:31.720
on "Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom."

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.860
I was called by the
makeup artist, Tom Smith.

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:37.780
And he said, "Would you come and do days?"

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:42.330
because someone, another
colleague had to go

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:44.223
and do pickups on a Bond-film.

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:47.340
And I said, oh, on Steven Spielberg film.

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:50.448
Oh, I don't think I can and
because my first thing was,

00:28:50.448 --> 00:28:51.281
"Oh, no, I'm not doing that."

00:28:51.281 --> 00:28:54.570
And he said, just get
your kit and get here

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.560
at five tomorrow morning.

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:00.160
I mean, it was just like,
okay, fine, turn up.

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:04.770
And it was just, I'll share a story.

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:08.340
I mean, I don't, it's not
something that I broadcast,

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:11.750
but I do remember that it was in the days

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:14.320
when there wasn't so much security.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:17.890
So I turn up at Elstree
Studios in North London

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:21.900
and I couldn't drive that times,
so I couldn't afford a car.

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:26.850
So I'd ordered a minicab, which
meant that for the journey,

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:30.122
it probably cost me half my day's pay

00:29:30.122 --> 00:29:32.945
(both chuckling)
to get there and back.

00:29:32.945 --> 00:29:33.778
- Yeah.

00:29:33.778 --> 00:29:37.130
- Anyway, 'cause it was
before the train started,

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:39.553
otherwise I'd have gone
on the train and the bus.

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:44.195
So it's not all glam. (scoffs)

00:29:44.195 --> 00:29:46.760
That's the other way of saying
it's a contrast, isn't it.

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:50.080
And I knew we were shooting
on, the makeup room.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:52.250
I got this so early, I got there about,

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:53.083
I don't know, quarter past four,

00:29:53.083 --> 00:29:54.963
cause I was keen and nervous,

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.770
that the makeup block was locked up.

00:29:57.770 --> 00:29:59.240
So there was no to go.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:01.750
So I'm wondering, and I
can see just a work light

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:04.440
on H-stage, which was
where the Temple of Doom

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:05.783
was actually constructed.

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:08.440
So I just walked through the door,

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:12.450
walked through the stage
door and walked onto the set.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:15.880
And I walked around and
the feeling inside me,

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:20.040
I mean, it makes my eyes
water now 'cause it was,

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:24.707
it was such an emotional
feeling of wonder and magic

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.034
and something so special.

00:30:29.034 --> 00:30:31.650
And I was walking through the tunnel

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.250
and there were two work
lights at either end,

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:36.830
just so there was a dark bit
in the middle and then two

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:37.663
pools of light.

00:30:37.663 --> 00:30:39.163
So very filmic anyway.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:42.573
And as I was walking through,

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:46.240
someone was walking in
the opposite direction

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:49.270
and I stopped in the pool of light

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:51.720
and they stopped in the pool of light,

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:53.970
and it was Steven.
- Hmm

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:56.000
- So it was a very magical thing.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.419
And he'd obviously gone to the stage.

00:30:58.419 --> 00:30:59.800
I don't think he even remembers it,

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:02.610
but he'd gone to the
stage to look at the set,

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:04.437
to work out what it was he
was going to do that day,

00:31:04.437 --> 00:31:06.895
because he's always prepared.

00:31:06.895 --> 00:31:10.160
I have never known him not be prepared.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.820
In the lunch time he's in
the editing room editing.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.883
He edits as he goes, he's
there at the makeup call

00:31:17.883 --> 00:31:21.883
editing, before the days
to start the day shooting.

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:27.740
He is a genius in that
he gets, he has a plan.

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:30.730
He has a vision in his
head and you are doing it

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:32.500
in that next moment.

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:34.790
But if something doesn't
work like the gimbal

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:38.220
on the landing craft, the
first day shooting on the beach

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:41.290
in "Saving Private Ryan,"
the gimbal wasn't working.

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:43.370
So he just turned around
and changed the shot,

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:45.260
change what he was going to do

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:48.060
and can be innovative in that way.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:51.630
He can think faster than
anyone else I know of

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:54.420
on a film set and come
up with something else.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:58.060
And you have to work to keep up with that.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:03.060
And when you present an idea
and he's really open to ideas

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:06.690
and you don't wanna get in
the way and he doesn't really

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:09.690
like makeup and hair
faffing around on the set,

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:10.790
that's how he sees it.
(Howard chuckles)

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:11.980
I mean, he wants the work done,

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:14.831
but somehow it's got to be
invisibly done by fairies

00:32:14.831 --> 00:32:18.020
rather than practically gluing a beard on.

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:20.270
I mean the amount of
time in the beginning,

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:21.627
I was just terrified, 'cause it would be,

00:32:21.627 --> 00:32:23.798
"Makeup, hey, get out," you know?

00:32:23.798 --> 00:32:27.567
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah!
- That's how it would be.

00:32:27.567 --> 00:32:31.120
And you know, I'd be shaking in my shoes,

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:33.240
but then I just learned
that you just duck and dive.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:35.780
So I'd just dip out of vision.

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:37.380
And then when he'd turn around to look

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:39.707
in the opposite direction,
I'd just pop up again.

00:32:39.707 --> 00:32:41.532
(Lois chortles)

00:32:41.532 --> 00:32:45.110
And you learn to do things really quickly,

00:32:45.110 --> 00:32:48.200
but you give him ideas
and he builds on it.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:50.740
So it's like be careful what you wish for,

00:32:50.740 --> 00:32:52.827
you come up with a good
idea and then suddenly

00:32:52.827 --> 00:32:55.840
instead of it being a little hill,

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:58.570
it's now Mount Everest
and you have to climb it

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:01.780
because you started that chain of thought.

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:04.210
But the one thing I will
tell you is that you are

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:09.210
a better technician and
artist at the end of that film

00:33:10.439 --> 00:33:12.303
than you were on day one.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:14.613
- Yeah.

00:33:14.613 --> 00:33:17.710
And the thing I love
about Steven, best of all,

00:33:17.710 --> 00:33:20.030
I mean, he is wonderful with
old people and children.

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:20.863
He really is.

00:33:20.863 --> 00:33:24.783
I mean, he's incredibly patient
and terribly kind actually.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:29.540
But when something goes right on that set

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:31.510
and you've got the take, even if it's been

00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:33.930
Billy Goat Gruff before
then, and it's been

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:37.890
slightly kind of impatient
and it's all been a bit

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.790
on the set bit stressful and everything,

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:42.187
but you get that shot and it's like,

00:33:42.187 --> 00:33:43.900
"Yes, yes, we've got it!"

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:47.310
And he's like a nine
year old or the first day

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:49.520
on the first film he's ever directed.

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:53.125
And that's, it's wonderful to behold.

00:33:53.125 --> 00:33:55.309
- Yeah, yup.

00:33:55.309 --> 00:33:57.620
- And to be a part of that magic,

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:01.750
even in a small way is fabulous.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:04.140
- Hm mm. Well I mean he truly loves film.

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:05.990
- Yeah.
- And that's something else,

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:09.110
I'm always surprised
when we have colleagues

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:12.637
and from any department and they go,

00:34:12.637 --> 00:34:13.940
"I'm not really into watching movie,

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:16.413
I'm not a big movie fan or watch movies."

00:34:16.413 --> 00:34:17.740
And I'm like, what are you doing this for?

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:18.690
- Yeah!

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:20.526
- The whole thing is about loving movies.

00:34:20.526 --> 00:34:23.217
I know you and I are
constantly texting each other,

00:34:23.217 --> 00:34:24.987
"Okay, I watched five movies tonight."

00:34:24.987 --> 00:34:26.057
"What did you watch?"

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:28.870
We're movie fanatics.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:30.680
We're cinephiles and I know he is too.

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:33.330
And when you talk about
him being resourceful,

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:36.570
I mean, look at "Jaws," he
saved that entire movie,

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:39.930
the shark didn't work, so
"Hey, let's just do POV!"

00:34:39.930 --> 00:34:43.953
And in our world and
every world I always feel

00:34:43.953 --> 00:34:46.000
like it's the work that we do is,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.450
we know how to do it, but
it's the problem solving

00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:51.265
and we have to be huge
problem solvers, you know?

00:34:51.265 --> 00:34:52.740
- Yeah.
- Huge.

00:34:52.740 --> 00:34:56.130
- And that actually that's the key to it.

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:58.430
I think that's the biggest key.

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:01.980
What do you do most of the
day once you've made them up?

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:02.813
Isn't that it?

00:35:02.813 --> 00:35:03.700
Well, of course that's never it

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:06.190
because it's like the fourth
bridge you paint one end

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:07.037
and then by the time
you've got to the end,

00:35:07.037 --> 00:35:08.680
you've gotta start all over again.

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:11.230
I mean, that's the nature of makeup,

00:35:11.230 --> 00:35:14.930
but the biggest job is problem solving

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:18.440
and it's coming up with
something and it can be,

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:22.170
I remember on "Catch Me If You Can,"

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:25.040
so there's a little boy in the hospital

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:29.090
then it's when Abagnaly, Aba...

00:35:30.327 --> 00:35:32.535
Abagnaly, isn't it?
- Yeah.

00:35:32.535 --> 00:35:35.870
Was pretending to be a
doctor in the hospital.

00:35:35.870 --> 00:35:39.910
So there's a little boy
and he's got a broken knee.

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:41.750
There's he's broken his knee or both,

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:44.630
had some kind of accident
anyway, some kind of trauma.

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:45.750
And he said it in the script.

00:35:45.750 --> 00:35:47.507
So I said, "Are we going to see this?"

00:35:47.507 --> 00:35:49.739
"No we need a prosthetic piece?"

00:35:49.739 --> 00:35:52.139
"No, we're never gonna see it."

00:35:52.139 --> 00:35:54.530
(Lois chuckles)
See it.

00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:58.799
So then Janusz, who is a diamond?

00:35:58.799 --> 00:36:01.790
I adore Janusz as cinematographer.

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:04.420
He is fabulous and he's
a really decent person.

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:07.930
Anyway, he said to me,
"You know we're starting

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:09.987
the shot on the kids' knee?"

00:36:09.987 --> 00:36:14.063
(Howard chortles)
I went, "What!"

00:36:14.063 --> 00:36:16.570
You know, so what you're gonna do?

00:36:16.570 --> 00:36:18.270
You can't let the set wait,

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:21.730
if you don't have a
prosthetic broken knee.

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:23.040
So I had to come up with something.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:26.000
So what I did was I
cut up a makeup sponge,

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:29.310
put wax around it, bled
it onto the kid's knee,

00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:31.450
dressed it with blood, and
that's what you are looking at

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:33.370
in a massive closeup.
- There you go.

00:36:33.370 --> 00:36:36.003
- Not something elaborately made.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:41.860
I once had to do a
lookalike for Prince Charles

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:43.610
on a film that had no money at all.

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:45.772
I mean, it was two bob and a toffee apple.

00:36:45.772 --> 00:36:48.090
It was really,
(Howard laughs)

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:50.920
you were trying to separate
the Kleenex into layers

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:52.490
to save money.
- Yeah! (laughs)

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:57.490
- It was one of those,
and his ears wouldn't be,

00:36:57.807 --> 00:37:02.260
and I didn't have the wherewithal,
a lab or anything else

00:37:02.260 --> 00:37:05.700
to give the push that
the ears actually needed.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:08.350
So yet again, I cut up, this is true,

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:12.690
I cut up a makeup sponge
and glued it, the wedge part

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:14.770
so that his ears, and it
was actually quite soft

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:16.650
and didn't hurt, but it
pushed the ears forward.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:19.780
- Hm mm.
- There was a magazine,

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.190
I don't know if it's available globally

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:24.560
or I don't even know if
it's still in existence,

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:26.690
to be perfectly honest, it's
called "Hello!" magazine.

00:37:26.690 --> 00:37:30.230
And they used to do visits
on sets and bits and bobs.

00:37:30.230 --> 00:37:32.650
And it was mainly celebrity gossip.

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:37.650
I mean, but sometimes a big
wedding or that kind of thing.

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:40.350
Anyway, something that people flip through

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:41.807
in the hair dresser's.

00:37:41.807 --> 00:37:46.807
So they came to interview
and I'm standing on the set.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:50.500
And the producer said,
"Of course you have to go

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:55.090
through the makeup trailer
and maybe you would want

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:58.740
to take some photographs
of our makeup artist, Lois,

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:02.742
applying Prince Charles' prosthetics.

00:38:02.742 --> 00:38:06.700
- (chuckles) Oh gosh!

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.650
- Yes, and the whole of my
life flashed before my eyes

00:38:09.650 --> 00:38:11.290
and I thought, "O my God, that's it."

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:14.120
I can't possibly have
anyone take a photograph

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:16.675
of cutting up makeup
sponge and gluing it on.

00:38:16.675 --> 00:38:18.082
- Gluing them back.
- Yeah!

00:38:18.082 --> 00:38:20.451
- Yeah. Well, but that's
part of what we do.

00:38:20.451 --> 00:38:23.723
Literally things, you
know, you plan and plan

00:38:23.723 --> 00:38:26.160
and production changes their mind

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:29.410
and directors change their
minds as it's their prerogative.

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:32.430
And it's our job to like, "Okay, alright,

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:33.263
I know what we're gonna do."

00:38:33.263 --> 00:38:35.260
Like you and I both have
the privilege of working

00:38:35.260 --> 00:38:37.900
with Danny Striepeke who was
a great, great makeup artist,

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:40.750
he's passed on but I think
of him every single day.

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:45.750
And he really pushed the
belief and I do agree with him

00:38:46.390 --> 00:38:48.670
that, and this is not ego-driven,

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:51.400
but we are the most
important people on set,

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:53.661
because we, he would
always say this, like,

00:38:53.661 --> 00:38:55.387
"We're the most important
people!" "We have to figure out."

00:38:55.387 --> 00:38:57.647
"We start the actor's
day." "We end the day."

00:38:57.647 --> 00:38:59.787
"They come in looking
terrible, we fix it."

00:38:59.787 --> 00:39:01.437
"There's a problem over here, we fix it."

00:39:01.437 --> 00:39:02.591
"We can do anything."

00:39:02.591 --> 00:39:05.499
And I really do believe
that because sometimes,

00:39:05.499 --> 00:39:10.499
as you have done, our job,
which we don't anticipate,

00:39:10.629 --> 00:39:12.410
goes into other departments

00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:15.310
and we have to help other
departments figure out,

00:39:15.310 --> 00:39:17.120
like I work a lot with costume.

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:19.107
I'm like, "Hmm, okay,
well, oh, I have an idea."

00:39:19.107 --> 00:39:19.940
"We can do that."

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:23.260
I know how to make some
costume look all bloody

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:24.340
without using blood.

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:26.250
So give me the costume
and I'll bring it back.

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:27.180
It'll be fine.

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:29.320
But let's talk a little bit about actors

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:31.930
because like I just
said, we start the day,

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:33.437
we start the actor's
day and we end the day

00:39:33.437 --> 00:39:36.813
and we always wanna make
sure that they feel safe

00:39:36.813 --> 00:39:40.110
and secure and confident.

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:43.660
So, because again, we've worked
with thousands and thousands

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:46.940
of actors and we don't have
to be specific about actors,

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:51.700
specific actors, but in the
sense of how do you go about,

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:54.110
you're gonna do a film
and you have an actor

00:39:54.110 --> 00:39:55.700
and let's say an A-list actor comes in

00:39:55.700 --> 00:39:57.300
and he's gonna be in your chair.

00:39:58.350 --> 00:40:02.240
How would you go about
having him feel comfortable

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:04.650
and confident and, and trusting you?

00:40:04.650 --> 00:40:09.650
'Cause we really do X
amount, as part of our job

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:12.554
and then the actor goes and
does the rest, you know?

00:40:12.554 --> 00:40:13.387
- Yeah.

00:40:13.387 --> 00:40:15.010
- But ultimately it's their
face on that 40 foot screen,

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:16.230
you know?
- That's right.

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:17.560
- But you know, you have to-

00:40:17.560 --> 00:40:20.280
- I think that, well, the
first thing I always do

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:22.930
is that if it's obviously a known actor,

00:40:22.930 --> 00:40:26.290
I will bone up on their filmography

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:30.070
and I will want to see
them either over the years

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:33.350
or them on a screen and
in different environments

00:40:33.350 --> 00:40:36.400
playing different roles
so that I actually have

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:41.130
a working knowledge inside
my mind of their face

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:46.130
with different expressions
and playing different roles.

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:51.260
So I can see, I suppose the
canvas, I get to know the canvas

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:56.560
that you're actually
working with as best I can.

00:41:00.059 --> 00:41:00.892
- Right.

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:07.270
- It's a difficult question
to answer because I literally,

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:09.980
it goes back to an another
answer to be honest,

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:11.680
I think I'm just myself.

00:41:11.680 --> 00:41:14.280
And what I don't do is I don't do

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:16.573
what I call fluff and fold.

00:41:16.573 --> 00:41:20.110
I think that there's a difference between,

00:41:20.110 --> 00:41:24.033
especially as a makeup artist, I think,

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:30.460
it can be seen as being a handmaid.

00:41:30.460 --> 00:41:32.050
It can be.

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:34.710
And that isn't something or a role

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:38.960
that I would ever wish anyone to be in

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:42.500
or would encourage them in order to make

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:45.163
an actor feel better or more special.

00:41:46.150 --> 00:41:47.902
Do you know what I mean?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:41:47.902 --> 00:41:49.926
- You have to find the boundary of that,

00:41:49.926 --> 00:41:51.700
but obviously you want to be kind

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:53.880
and if someone has a
problem with something,

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:57.370
they've got spots or
whatever it happens to be,

00:41:57.370 --> 00:41:59.800
a blemish and it worries them,

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:04.700
then you are considerate
and you solve the problem

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:06.920
and make them feel better
about it, of course,

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:08.080
but you don't lie.

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:10.690
I think that's the most important thing.

00:42:10.690 --> 00:42:12.100
Never be untruthful.

00:42:12.100 --> 00:42:15.050
If you can't actually speak
the truth, say nothing.

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:16.050
(Lois laughs)

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.562
I think that that's a rule of thumb

00:42:18.562 --> 00:42:23.562
and I don't talk about myself
whenever I meet an actor.

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:29.340
I talk about the work as
if the work is something

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:33.720
that is there that unites both of us.

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.830
And that's our meeting point.

00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:40.320
It's not my personality
or their personality.

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:41.220
It's the work.

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:45.660
And that's, I think how
you create confidence

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:48.241
and an ability to work with each other.

00:42:48.241 --> 00:42:51.490
There was an actor who
shall remain nameless,

00:42:51.490 --> 00:42:54.610
who was truthful enough actually,

00:42:54.610 --> 00:42:57.637
although it took a little
bit of work to say,

00:42:57.637 --> 00:43:01.790
"I really do not want, and I
would not have taken this role

00:43:01.790 --> 00:43:05.117
if I thought that I had to have makeup,

00:43:05.117 --> 00:43:09.177
I would not have taken this role."

00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:12.737
And I said, "I'm so terribly sorry."

00:43:12.737 --> 00:43:16.970
"But the thing is that we are
both in service of this film

00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:21.970
and we are going to need
you to be in this makeup

00:43:22.517 --> 00:43:26.033
in order to give the director his vision."

00:43:26.033 --> 00:43:31.000
"And in fact, fulfill
the remit from both of us

00:43:31.900 --> 00:43:35.377
to tell the story of this
film through your character."

00:43:35.377 --> 00:43:40.377
"And what we need to do is find a place

00:43:41.191 --> 00:43:45.629
where you are not hampered
in your performance

00:43:45.629 --> 00:43:50.629
by the consciousness or the
physicality of the makeup

00:43:51.089 --> 00:43:55.480
and I can still for fulfill the remit

00:43:56.970 --> 00:44:00.917
of the director's vision
and indeed the script."

00:44:00.917 --> 00:44:04.820
"So we have to find where we can dovetail

00:44:04.820 --> 00:44:06.470
and where we can meet."

00:44:06.470 --> 00:44:08.020
And I could have been replaced

00:44:08.020 --> 00:44:10.070
because they're not
gonna replace the actor.

00:44:10.070 --> 00:44:12.829
They're gonna replace the makeup artist.

00:44:12.829 --> 00:44:13.680
- Hmm.

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:16.490
- But it's very important to be truthful

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:19.573
and that's not ego
driven, it's just factual.

00:44:20.890 --> 00:44:22.800
- Yeah, no, it's true.

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:25.550
It's true. And, and I think that's smart.

00:44:25.550 --> 00:44:28.940
That's a good, another
great piece of advice is,

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:30.870
don't make it about yourself.

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:32.455
You know, you're there.

00:44:32.455 --> 00:44:34.640
And there is important to
keep a line in the sand

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.530
between personal and business,

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:39.820
and I think sometimes that
gets a little mixed up

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:42.350
and you compromise the actor too,

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:43.650
because then the actor feels like

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:46.110
there may be a little misinterpretation

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:49.030
of a personal relationship,
just a friendship,

00:44:49.030 --> 00:44:50.690
but you don't want to go there.

00:44:50.690 --> 00:44:52.403
You're there to do a
job and do the best job.

00:44:52.403 --> 00:44:53.730
And, and they are too.

00:44:53.730 --> 00:44:54.980
They're not there to be your best friend,

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:56.639
if you want a best friend, get a dog.

00:44:56.639 --> 00:44:58.100
- Yeah.
- And that'll be great.

00:44:58.100 --> 00:44:59.920
Or a cat.
- Yeah.

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:01.835
Or a cat pillow like you have behind you.

00:45:01.835 --> 00:45:03.910
(both chuckling)

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:07.540
But yeah that's a very important
thing 'cause I think also

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:09.100
people that are up and
coming in the industry

00:45:09.100 --> 00:45:12.057
and in our world sometimes
get that confused,

00:45:12.057 --> 00:45:15.880
try to dig in and become friends

00:45:15.880 --> 00:45:17.920
and that's not what you're there for.

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:20.450
- No, and that can blow up so easily.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:22.363
I mean on both sides as well.

00:45:23.450 --> 00:45:27.670
Because the thing is
there's the give and take.

00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:30.290
And there has to be a
point, even if you've worked

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:32.711
and I've been privileged
enough to work with

00:45:32.711 --> 00:45:35.780
some wonderful actors on numerous films

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:37.490
and be requested by them.

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:39.980
And it's always a surprise.

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:41.771
It's never an expectation.

00:45:41.771 --> 00:45:45.443
Always thrilled to be
asked for. It's lovely.

00:45:48.349 --> 00:45:51.137
But there comes a point
where you have to say,

00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:55.560
you know what, I know you want this,

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:58.863
but this is not in your
service in the end,

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:03.290
you need to look like X, not Y-

00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:05.320
- Hm mm, hm mm, hm mm.

00:46:05.320 --> 00:46:08.120
- because I think you'll find,

00:46:08.120 --> 00:46:10.740
so you have to be able to have that ground

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:14.710
of professional relationship to negotiate.

00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:15.723
- Hm mm.

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:17.780
- It can't be-
- I agree.

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:20.696
- oh, it's Lois again, doing it.

00:46:20.696 --> 00:46:22.183
They have thought that.

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:26.660
I mean, I remember once (chuckles)

00:46:26.660 --> 00:46:28.300
there was an actor in my chair

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:32.440
and obviously we've worked
together, you know, quite a bit.

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:35.533
And it was one of those days where,

00:46:37.190 --> 00:46:38.810
gone now obviously with COVID,

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:40.330
the one good thing about COVID

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:42.940
is there's no food in the
trailer, thanks to God, right?

00:46:42.940 --> 00:46:43.773
- Yes.

00:46:43.773 --> 00:46:45.697
- 'Cause that's nothing
more irritating than that,

00:46:45.697 --> 00:46:47.600
but it was one of those days where

00:46:47.600 --> 00:46:50.630
we are on the phone, someone's
having a conversation

00:46:50.630 --> 00:46:53.623
standing in the doorway of the trailer,

00:46:55.580 --> 00:46:57.750
we're on the phone, someone's standing in

00:46:57.750 --> 00:47:00.280
or popping in and out
with different updates.

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:05.280
So it's constantly moving
and then there's chewing gum

00:47:07.750 --> 00:47:11.410
and then the headset went in and all I did

00:47:11.410 --> 00:47:16.410
I went (sighs) because I've
got 10 minutes to do this.

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:22.793
I'm going to throw myself
out the window if I do this.

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:30.250
I was kind of up to here, and
the, the ear bobbles came out,

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:34.163
the chewing stopped and the actor said,

00:47:36.877 --> 00:47:38.467
"What really upset you?"

00:47:38.467 --> 00:47:40.800
"Was it someone coming and out,

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:43.563
the (smacks lips) chewing of the gum,

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:47.111
phone calls?" and I went, "All of it."

00:47:47.111 --> 00:47:50.065
(both chuckling)

00:47:50.065 --> 00:47:53.065
But that's being a person, you know?

00:47:54.027 --> 00:47:54.860
- Yeah.

00:47:54.860 --> 00:47:59.180
- And yeah, we all have
our frailties and yeah.

00:48:00.543 --> 00:48:03.088
- Yeah, and, and hopefully
at the end of the day,

00:48:03.088 --> 00:48:06.720
you do a great job and the actor is happy

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:09.530
and like you said, every job I end,

00:48:09.530 --> 00:48:11.880
I can be doing a film with an actor

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:14.020
or films with an actor multiple times,

00:48:14.020 --> 00:48:15.600
I never expect a phone call.

00:48:15.600 --> 00:48:17.357
I just go, "Thank you so
much for the opportunity."

00:48:17.357 --> 00:48:21.594
"It was great and best of luck."

00:48:21.594 --> 00:48:24.060
And then if they call you,
"Wow, that's really nice."

00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:25.070
It's a nice surprise.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:28.877
It's just part of the gravy,
but never ever feel like,

00:48:28.877 --> 00:48:30.880
"Oh yeah, I'm gonna be
doing this guy forever."

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:33.676
That doesn't exist. It will never exist.

00:48:33.676 --> 00:48:34.509
- Yeah.

00:48:34.509 --> 00:48:36.060
- So those of you that are
getting into the industry,

00:48:36.060 --> 00:48:38.960
please remember that, it's
just, you're there to do the job

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:40.020
and do the best job you can.

00:48:40.020 --> 00:48:42.873
Be super friendly to everybody
and all that good stuff.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:46.210
I have a question, a
technical question actually,

00:48:46.210 --> 00:48:48.570
now that now that filmmaking
has changed so much,

00:48:48.570 --> 00:48:50.870
now that we're not
dealing so much with film,

00:48:50.870 --> 00:48:52.910
there are some filmmakers
that still use film,

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:56.829
but mostly digital, have
you found that it's,

00:48:56.829 --> 00:48:59.160
have you come up with a
process where you're dealing

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:02.730
with being able to work
with different skin tones?

00:49:02.730 --> 00:49:05.130
Because also our films
are getting more diverse,

00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:08.230
which is great, our actors
are far more diverse.

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:10.070
It's not what we are used to.

00:49:10.070 --> 00:49:11.880
We're really opening up the, the gates

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:15.129
and everybody is inclusive
in filmmaking now.

00:49:15.129 --> 00:49:20.129
Have you had to learn more
about things like this

00:49:20.570 --> 00:49:22.800
or how do you approach
it now, in this world?

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:25.120
Well to be perfectly honest,
I think that everything,

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:28.260
especially with makeup comes
down to the color wheel.

00:49:28.260 --> 00:49:29.450
I mean, that's what it comes down for.

00:49:29.450 --> 00:49:31.800
You have to know what the
color wheel is, right.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:33.805
And tones within that.

00:49:33.805 --> 00:49:38.493
When I was first starting out,

00:49:39.450 --> 00:49:41.579
you had to be able to make up everyone

00:49:41.579 --> 00:49:44.502
to look in all different ways.

00:49:44.502 --> 00:49:45.569
- Right.

00:49:45.569 --> 00:49:50.569
- For example there're
stuntmen men who were little,

00:49:51.260 --> 00:49:54.720
there was a little person
who I worked with regularly,

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:56.720
actually for a whole, called Kiran Shah.

00:49:58.542 --> 00:49:59.502
- Yeah, Kiran Shah.

00:49:59.502 --> 00:50:03.010
- Who's a great stunt
man and stunt coordinator

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:05.040
and a fabulous personality.

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:07.130
Well I've turned Kiran into everything

00:50:07.130 --> 00:50:11.920
from a green fairy in "Legend,"

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:16.920
through to a washer woman
for a perspective double,

00:50:17.820 --> 00:50:22.820
to an Edwardian child,
Caucasian Edwardian child

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:30.840
through to, well, I'm trying
to think the last time,

00:50:31.570 --> 00:50:34.590
I mean, he's been a pixie, a goblin,

00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:36.450
he's been every shade-
- Yeah!

00:50:36.450 --> 00:50:38.980
- of skin tone you can imagine.

00:50:38.980 --> 00:50:42.958
So I don't think that that has changed.

00:50:42.958 --> 00:50:47.958
They're the arrows that are
in our quiver, of make up.

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:51.418
So I do think what's happened

00:50:51.418 --> 00:50:54.320
and I'm quite relieved about it actually,

00:50:54.320 --> 00:50:59.320
is that it's less, I think
makeup is more subtle.

00:51:00.161 --> 00:51:02.730
- Hm mm, yes.
- And I like that.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:06.370
I've always enjoyed and want to use

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:09.750
whenever is possible, obviously, raw skin,

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:12.830
even in film days before digital,

00:51:12.830 --> 00:51:15.360
I like raw skin I like how it looks.

00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:20.183
I think it makes people
just somehow subliminally

00:51:20.183 --> 00:51:22.820
you recognize that it's another person,

00:51:22.820 --> 00:51:25.100
not a made up person in front of you.

00:51:25.100 --> 00:51:28.390
So for all those reasons, I've
always been a fan of that.

00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:30.920
So it hasn't really affected me that much.

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:34.040
I think it might have affected
some of my colleagues more

00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:38.810
that are more inclined to
foundation being their go-to.

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:40.350
- Right.

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:42.850
- I mean, I like to do things
like paint on freckles,

00:51:42.850 --> 00:51:44.371
you know?
- Yeah.

00:51:44.371 --> 00:51:45.900
I've always liked to do
that, to create characters,

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:47.723
those sort of things. So-

00:51:49.050 --> 00:51:49.883
- Yeah, I know.

00:51:49.883 --> 00:51:50.716
That's great. And that's the detail.

00:51:50.716 --> 00:51:51.549
Yeah. I agree with you.

00:51:51.549 --> 00:51:55.250
I think less is more, especially on men.

00:51:55.250 --> 00:51:57.399
It's, really just taking, being observant.

00:51:57.399 --> 00:51:58.291
- Yeah.

00:51:58.291 --> 00:51:59.124
- Knowing your color wheel,

00:51:59.124 --> 00:52:00.080
then knowing what skin looks like.

00:52:00.080 --> 00:52:02.110
I feel like if you, if
you know those things,

00:52:02.110 --> 00:52:03.590
you're good to go with everything.

00:52:03.590 --> 00:52:04.797
- Exactly.

00:52:04.797 --> 00:52:06.640
- And yeah just pay
attention when you're on set.

00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:08.970
Look at the monitor. Maybe
look through the lens.

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:11.760
- Well then yeah. But trust your eye.

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:13.045
I mean,-
- And trust the eye.

00:52:13.045 --> 00:52:13.989
- And that's the thing.

00:52:13.989 --> 00:52:16.260
I think now of everything,
to be perfectly honest,

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:19.350
how I think that you can
technically teach people

00:52:19.350 --> 00:52:20.330
how to do things.

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:23.320
You can always teach
people how to do things,

00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:25.020
practical tasks.

00:52:25.020 --> 00:52:28.840
This is how you block a wig.
This is how you block a beard.

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:32.138
Except that's really
not taught. (chuckles)

00:52:32.138 --> 00:52:33.130
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:34.940
- But what you can't teach anyone.

00:52:34.940 --> 00:52:37.490
And I've never known
it be able to be taught

00:52:37.490 --> 00:52:42.490
is to have an eye, the
eye for either observation

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:48.520
or recreation of something, that ability

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:51.304
to look at something and see,

00:52:51.304 --> 00:52:54.850
like the like native peoples,

00:52:54.850 --> 00:52:58.182
when they did sculptures from a stone

00:52:58.182 --> 00:53:03.090
that the sculpture is already
in existence within the stone,

00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:05.670
you just take away the debris to reveal

00:53:05.670 --> 00:53:08.370
what was already there.
- Hm mm.

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:09.820
- That.
- Hm mm.

00:53:09.820 --> 00:53:14.240
- And that in our job is the difference

00:53:14.240 --> 00:53:16.930
between night and day.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:20.700
If someone has that ability,
that you look at a face

00:53:20.700 --> 00:53:23.750
and you don't see the
face in front of you,

00:53:23.750 --> 00:53:26.840
you see who and what that face can become.

00:53:26.840 --> 00:53:27.980
- Right!

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:30.440
You've been a member of the
Academy of Motion Picture

00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:32.230
Arts and Science for quite a while.

00:53:32.230 --> 00:53:33.280
What does that mean to you?

00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:35.010
What does it mean to you to be a member?

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:38.250
Do you remember being
invited into the Academy

00:53:38.250 --> 00:53:39.710
and what that felt like?

00:53:39.710 --> 00:53:42.500
- Well there are three things actually

00:53:42.500 --> 00:53:46.245
that all come together because
one leads to the other.

00:53:46.245 --> 00:53:51.245
And I'm going to tell you
a little story if I may,

00:53:52.880 --> 00:53:53.976
if there's time for it.

00:53:53.976 --> 00:53:54.809
- Yes?

00:53:56.340 --> 00:54:01.340
- When I was an officer and
on the board of the Academy,

00:54:03.520 --> 00:54:08.520
they were doing a retrospective
of "Grease" and I was there,

00:54:09.810 --> 00:54:12.040
and there were lots of
other people as well,

00:54:12.040 --> 00:54:14.750
but there was John Travolta
and Olivia Newton John,

00:54:14.750 --> 00:54:17.400
there they were, they were
like feet in front of me.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:21.310
Now when I was, so I
suppose a late teenager,

00:54:21.310 --> 00:54:23.626
I can't remember how old I was but,

00:54:23.626 --> 00:54:27.560
but I went to see "Grease"
In the Odeon Muswell Hill

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:31.727
'cause I lived in North
London with my friend Marina,

00:54:31.727 --> 00:54:36.727
and we came out of that and
danced our way to the bus,

00:54:38.116 --> 00:54:42.040
to go home and as I sat in the Goldwyn,

00:54:42.040 --> 00:54:46.000
which is the most
magnificent cinema to me,

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:51.000
it's the Mecca of cinemas,
in the row in front of me

00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:56.283
was Olivia Newton John and John Travolta.

00:54:57.220 --> 00:55:01.080
And as the curtain opened and you saw

00:55:01.080 --> 00:55:03.070
the beginning of the film.
- Yeah?

00:55:03.070 --> 00:55:06.010
I suddenly had this feeling of the past

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:09.230
and the present colliding.

00:55:09.230 --> 00:55:14.230
That young woman had
no idea that she would

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:18.244
one day have her bottom
parked in the Goldwyn,

00:55:18.244 --> 00:55:21.726
(Howard chuckles)
be an Academy member

00:55:21.726 --> 00:55:24.530
and work in Hollywood.

00:55:24.530 --> 00:55:28.873
It was somehow like going to the moon,

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:32.120
something that was unbelievable,

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:34.230
which of course, now we can
do, if you've got enough money,

00:55:34.230 --> 00:55:37.274
you can pay to go, but you know, it's-

00:55:37.274 --> 00:55:38.107
- Yeah.

00:55:38.107 --> 00:55:39.234
- It was that.

00:55:39.234 --> 00:55:43.470
So in a way it's always
been mystical to me

00:55:43.470 --> 00:55:48.470
and somehow treasured
and beyond comprehension

00:55:49.590 --> 00:55:54.590
in a way, rather like saying,
"I have an Academy Award."

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:57.810
- Right.
- That alone is so rare.

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:01.940
It's such a rarity and it's somehow

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:03.880
almost nothing to do with me.

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:07.400
It's an external factor, but I tell you

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:12.400
what is really special, I
feel, is to be in service.

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:15.806
I love to be in service.

00:56:15.806 --> 00:56:16.970
- Yes.

00:56:16.970 --> 00:56:20.250
- I like to be in service
of something bigger than me.

00:56:20.250 --> 00:56:23.960
I like that about filmmaking
because the project

00:56:23.960 --> 00:56:25.486
that's bigger than you.

00:56:25.486 --> 00:56:30.200
I like it about the
Academy because it's about

00:56:30.200 --> 00:56:33.790
the other 364 days of the year,

00:56:33.790 --> 00:56:37.577
as well as the Academy Awards.

00:56:37.577 --> 00:56:42.260
So that, I think it's a privilege.

00:56:42.260 --> 00:56:44.790
I think it's something you need to take,

00:56:44.790 --> 00:56:47.800
I take terribly seriously
and also lightheartedly.

00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:51.390
I mean I always think that one
of the most important things,

00:56:51.390 --> 00:56:53.940
not that I could use it now,
because of the COVID protocols,

00:56:53.940 --> 00:56:56.180
but one of the most important things

00:56:56.180 --> 00:57:00.040
I've always had in my kit
on any film I've ever done

00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:02.918
is what I call the attitude adjuster.

00:57:02.918 --> 00:57:06.872
And the attitude adjuster
is a clown's red nose.

00:57:06.872 --> 00:57:10.164
(Howard laughs)
- I like that.

00:57:10.164 --> 00:57:14.250
- And it's important because
you take things seriously

00:57:14.250 --> 00:57:16.030
and lightly at the same time.

00:57:16.030 --> 00:57:17.750
- Hm mm. That's true.

00:57:17.750 --> 00:57:19.640
Well, Lois, this has been wonderful.

00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:23.030
Thank you so much for your
time and your words of wisdom.

00:57:23.030 --> 00:57:25.000
And I hope that everyone watching this

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:28.900
walks away with a handful of great advice

00:57:28.900 --> 00:57:32.546
and encouragement, because
everything is possible.

00:57:32.546 --> 00:57:34.874
Like you said about Olivia Newton John,

00:57:34.874 --> 00:57:37.210
when we were younger,
we never had any thought

00:57:37.210 --> 00:57:38.130
we'd be here today.

00:57:38.130 --> 00:57:40.180
Like be really be in the industry

00:57:40.180 --> 00:57:43.630
or be where we are or
be in the Academy or,

00:57:43.630 --> 00:57:44.463
or all of it.

00:57:44.463 --> 00:57:47.810
Just being in the film
industry is a privilege.

00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:50.440
So Lois, once again, wonderful.

00:57:50.440 --> 00:57:51.830
I love speaking with you.

00:57:51.830 --> 00:57:53.720
Hey everyone, thank you for participating

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:56.560
in the Academy's Visiting
Artists Film series,

00:57:56.560 --> 00:57:59.620
Film Aid Edition for makeup and hair.

00:57:59.620 --> 00:58:01.253
Hope you enjoyed it. Thank you.

